ass=3D"u-margin-s-bottom">Social capital=E2=80=94the strength of an individ=
ual=E2=80=99s social network and community=E2=80=94has been identified as a=
 potential determinant of outcomes ranging from education to health<a class=
=3D"anchor anchor-primary" href=3D"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/ch=
apter/handbook/abs/pii/B9780444534293000041#CR1" name=3D"bCR1" data-sd-ui-s=
ide-panel-opener=3D"true" data-xocs-content-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-co=
ntent-id=3D"CR1"><span class=3D"anchor-text-container"><span class=3D"ancho=
r-text">1</span></span></a>=E2=80=93<a class=3D"anchor anchor-primary" href=
=3D"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/B9780444=
534293000041#CR8" name=3D"bCR8" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener=3D"true" data-=
xocs-content-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-content-id=3D"CR8"><span class=3D=
"anchor-text-container"><span class=3D"anchor-text">8</span></span></a>. Ho=
wever, efforts to understand what types of social capital matter for these =
outcomes have been hindered by a lack of social network data. Here, in the =
first of a pair of papers<a class=3D"anchor anchor-primary" href=3D"https:/=
/www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/B978044453429300004=
1#CR9" name=3D"bCR9" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener=3D"true" data-xocs-conten=
t-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-content-id=3D"CR9"><span class=3D"anchor-tex=
t-container"><span class=3D"anchor-text">9</span></span></a>, we use data o=
n 21=E2=80=89billion friendships from Facebook to study social capital. We =
measure and analyse three types of social capital by ZIP (postal) code in t=
he United States: (1) connectedness between different types of people, such=
 as those with low versus high socioeconomic status (SES); (2) social cohes=
ion, such as the extent of cliques in friendship networks; and (3) civic en=
gagement, such as rates of volunteering. These measures vary substantially =
across areas, but are not highly correlated with each other. We demonstrate=
 the importance of distinguishing these forms of social capital by analysin=
g their associations with economic mobility across areas. The share of high=
-SES friends among individuals with low SES=E2=80=94which we term economic =
connectedness=E2=80=94is among the strongest predictors of upward income mo=
bility identified to date<a class=3D"anchor anchor-primary" href=3D"https:/=
/www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/B978044453429300004=
1#CR10" name=3D"bCR10" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener=3D"true" data-xocs-cont=
ent-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-content-id=3D"CR10"><span class=3D"anchor-=
text-container"><span class=3D"anchor-text">10</span></span></a>,<a class=
=3D"anchor anchor-primary" href=3D"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/ch=
apter/handbook/abs/pii/B9780444534293000041#CR11" name=3D"bCR11" data-sd-ui=
-side-panel-opener=3D"true" data-xocs-content-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-=
content-id=3D"CR11"><span class=3D"anchor-text-container"><span class=3D"an=
chor-text">11</span></span></a>. Other social capital measures are not stro=
ngly associated with economic mobility. If children with low-SES parents we=
re to grow up in counties with economic connectedness comparable to that of=
 the average child with high-SES parents, their incomes in adulthood would =
increase by 20% on average. Differences in economic connectedness can expla=
in well-known relationships between upward income mobility and racial segre=
gation, poverty rates, and inequality<a class=3D"anchor anchor-primary" hre=
f=3D"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/chapter/handbook/abs/pii/B978044=
4534293000041#CR12" name=3D"bCR12" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener=3D"true" da=
ta-xocs-content-type=3D"reference" data-xocs-content-id=3D"CR12"><span clas=
s=3D"anchor-text-container"><span class=3D"anchor-text">12</span></span></a=
